# PROJECT: CHOIR MANAGEMENT, ATTENDANCE, DISCIPLINE AND FINANCIAL ACCOUNTABILITY SYSTEM

## ROLE AND EXPECTATIONS

Act as a senior software architect, Google Workspace engineer, database designer, Google Apps Script specialist, financial record-management specialist and user-experience designer.

Your task is to design and implement a complete digital management system for a church choir with approximately 30–50 members.

This is a practical management system that will be operated primarily by the Provost, with oversight from the Choir Master and authorised executives.

I want a professional, reliable, automated, transparent and easy-to-use system.

I am not a professional software developer. The system must therefore be simple enough for a non-technical choir executive to operate from a smartphone or laptop.

Do not merely describe what could be built. Produce a complete, implementable solution.

# PART 1: NON-NEGOTIABLE FINANCIAL AND TECHNOLOGY CONSTRAINTS

## 1. Zero-budget implementation

The choir currently has no dedicated funding for software development, WhatsApp API subscriptions, hosting or third-party services.

Therefore, the first version MUST use:

- Google Sheets.
- Google Forms.
- Google Apps Script.
- Google Drive.

Use a standard personal Google account wherever possible.

Do not introduce:

- Paid WhatsApp API services.
- Paid chatbot providers.
- Paid web hosting.
- Firebase services that require billing.
- Paid database subscriptions.
- AppSheet subscriptions.
- Paid third-party automation platforms.
- Unnecessary external APIs.
- Paid QR-code services.

The solution must avoid recurring software charges.

Where a proposed feature requires a paid service, do not implement it. Explain the limitation and provide a free alternative.

Use Google's existing services and stay within their applicable free-account quotas.

Do not activate any Google Cloud billing account or paid service.

## 2. WhatsApp is a future feature, NOT part of Phase 1

A WhatsApp chatbot may eventually be introduced, but it is not currently funded.

Do not build, deploy or activate a WhatsApp Business API integration in the initial version.

However, design the database and architecture so that a future WhatsApp bot can connect without requiring the entire system to be rebuilt.

This means:

- Every chorister must have a permanent unique Member ID.
- Every rehearsal must have a unique Session ID.
- Every attendance entry must have a unique Attendance ID.
- Every fine must have a unique Fine ID.
- Every payment must have a unique Payment ID.
- Every complaint, query and application must have a unique reference number.
- Store phone numbers in a standardised format, preferably international format.
- Maintain a clean and consistent database structure.
- Separate the database from the user interface.
- Keep business rules centralised rather than embedding them only in individual forms.

Create a documented future integration plan explaining how WhatsApp could later connect to the same database.

Do not allow a future WhatsApp integration requirement to delay or complicate the initial system.

